CameraRig.description:
The rig on the camera entity. `rig` is the authored configuration (writing a different value
starts a timed blend), `shake` survives switches, `trauma`, `grounded` and `look_deg` are
game-written inputs, `state` is engine-written. The kick, roll and FOV channels are game-written
too (the engine decays only the two kicks) and apply after aim. Every field defaults, so older documents keep loading.

# Example

```
use ludion_camera::{CameraRig, RigMode};

let rig: CameraRig = serde_json::from_value(serde_json::json!({
    "rig": { "mode": { "track": { "path": "paths/behind-1.path.yaml", "offset": [0.0, 2.2, 4.5] } } },
    "trauma": 0.25,
}))
.unwrap();
let RigMode::Track(track) = &rig.rig.mode else { unreachable!() };
assert_eq!(track.offset, [0.0, 2.2, 4.5]);
assert_eq!(rig.rig.springs.position_half_life, 0.15);
```

look_deg.description:
Look input `[yaw, pitch]` in degrees (positive yaw turns left, positive pitch looks down)
for `first_person` and `chase`; `follow` consumes and discards it. The game writes it,
usually through [`add_look`]; the rig applies and zeroes it on each tick it poses.

CameraZone.description:
A sensor volume that drives the scene's [`CameraRig`] while the rig's target origin is inside
it. Put it beside a `Collider` with `sensor: true`; the highest `priority` among the zones the
target stands in wins, the lowest entity id breaking a tie.

```
use ludion_camera::CameraZone;

let zone: CameraZone = serde_json::from_value(serde_json::json!({
    "priority": 2,
    "rig": { "focus_offset": [0.0, 1.5, 0.0] },
}))
.unwrap();
assert_eq!(zone.priority, 2);
assert_eq!(zone.rig.focus_offset, [0.0, 1.5, 0.0]);
```

ContactMaterial.description:
The contact material of a `static` or `kinematic` collider: friction, restitution and their
combine rules. A [`Body::Rigid`] body carries the same four fields on [`RigidBody`].

```yaml
body: { static: { restitution: 0.3 } }
body: { kinematic: { friction_combine: max } }
```

GroundMarkerClip.description:
Clips the `GroundMarker` beside it by the walls its apex can see. `bins` rays fan across the
marker's arc (the full circle for a disc or ring) at `height` above the entity origin; the marker
is drawn only out to the first hit per ray. A `partial_layers` hit does not clip but dims what lies
behind it to `partial_alpha`. `layers` empty means every layer; `partial_layers` empty means none.
Static colliders only unless `dynamic`, which casts every extract.

```
use ludion_components::GroundMarkerClip;

let clip: GroundMarkerClip = serde_json::from_value(serde_json::json!({
    "bins": 128,
    "layers": ["sight_blocker"],
    "partial_layers": ["low_cover"],
}))
.unwrap();
assert_eq!(clip.height, 1.6);
assert_eq!(clip.partial_alpha, 0.35);
assert!(!clip.dynamic);
```

LayerSet.description:
An ordered list of layer names, in the author's order (never sorted, so a scene's text does not
churn). Serializes as a plain list of strings.

```
use ludion_physics::LayerSet;

let set = LayerSet::from(vec!["light_occluder".to_string()]);
assert_eq!(set.as_slice(), ["light_occluder"]);
assert!(!set.is_empty());
assert_eq!(serde_json::to_string(&set).unwrap(), r#"["light_occluder"]"#);
```

IgnoreCollisions.description:
Makes a motor's own queries ignore every collider `with` names: its sweeps, ground probes,
posture and resize tests and [`character_fit`](crate::character_fit). A paired action needs it,
because two capsules standing at a 0.5 m choke overlap; each participant of an N-role action
lists all the others.

It is read at query time and is not part of the physics blob. It does not stop the solver from
pushing a dynamic body that `with` names; it only changes what the motor sees. Without the
component, or with an empty list, every motor result is unchanged. A motor looks through at most
[`MAX_PARTNERS`] (4) entities: a longer list uses its first four at runtime and is `L1759` in a
document.

```
use ludion_core::ids::EntityId;
use ludion_physics::IgnoreCollisions;

let partner = EntityId::new(7, 0);
let ignore = IgnoreCollisions { with: vec![partner] };
assert_eq!(ignore.clone(), ignore);
```

IkTargets.description:
Targets for a voxel assembly's `ik:` entries, keyed by the entry's name.

A chain reaches its target with its effector; a look-at turns its aim parts so `forward` faces
it. Each target sets at most one of `point`, `entity` (with an `offset` in its frame) or
`direction` (`L1673`); `weight` blends from the animated pose to the solved one and ramps over
`fade` seconds from `changed`. The map is an entity-reference collection, so a despawned `entity`
is nulled on save and carry.

**Presentation only.** Extract solves the IK after the clip and its layers; no system reads
the component, and a scene without it poses exactly as before. It is ordinary hashed sim state,
so write it only when a choice changes (an `entity` target follows with no write). An entry with
no target, or at weight `0`, is not solved. An assembly without `ik:` ignores the component.

```yaml
components:
  IkTargets:
    targets:
      look:
        direction: { yaw_deg: 30.0 }
        weight: 1.0
      hand_r:
        point: [2.0, 1.0, -3.0]
        weight: 1.0
      hand_l:
        entity: { index: 42, generation: 0 }
        offset: [0.0, 0.1, 0.0]
        weight: 1.0
```

```
use ludion_components::{IkTarget, IkTargets, fade_ik_target, ik_target_weight};
use ludion_core::time::{SimTime, TickRate};

let mut ik = IkTargets::default();
ik.targets.insert("look".into(), IkTarget { point: Some([0.0, 1.0, -3.0]), ..Default::default() });
let now = SimTime::from_ticks(60, TickRate::DEFAULT);
assert!(fade_ik_target(&mut ik, "look", 1.0, 0.5, now));
// Half the fade later the target is half on.
let later = SimTime::from_ticks(75, TickRate::DEFAULT);
assert_eq!(ik_target_weight(&ik.targets["look"], later), 0.5);
```

Look.description:
A facing offset from the entity's own rotation, in degrees. Yaw 0 looks along the entity's −Z,
**positive yaw turns left** and **positive pitch looks down**: the camera rig's convention.

```
use ludion_components::Look;

let look: Look = serde_json::from_value(serde_json::json!({ "pitch_deg": 10.0 })).unwrap();
assert_eq!(look, Look { yaw_deg: 0.0, pitch_deg: 10.0 });
```

LaneConstraint.description:
Holds a [`CharacterMotor`](ludion_physics::CharacterMotor) to a `kind: path` document: the
character's displacement is projected onto the path's horizontal direction and a pull keeps it
on the centre line, eased by `blend`. An entity needs a `CharacterMotor` for the lane to be read
(`L1302`).

`s` and `blend` are the lane's own state, written by `move_character`; a game writes `engaged`.
Changing `path` at runtime must also null `s`: use [`set_path`](Self::set_path).

# Example
```
use ludion_components::{LaneConstraint, LaneEnds};

let lane: LaneConstraint = serde_json::from_value(serde_json::json!({
    "path": "paths/corridor.path.yaml",
    "engaged": true,
    "ends": "stop",
}))
.unwrap();
assert!(lane.engaged);
assert_eq!(lane.ends, LaneEnds::Stop);
assert_eq!(lane.s, None);
```

LightExposure.description:
A subject whose light exposure `light_exposure_update` reads every tick into
`LightExposureResults`. `points` are **entity-local offsets**, mapped through the entity's world
pose; `facing.normal` is world-space. `occluder_layers` absent means static colliders only (the
subject excluded); present means exactly those layers.

```
use ludion_components::{Combine, Facing, LightExposure};

let subject: LightExposure = serde_json::from_value(serde_json::json!({
    "points": [[0.0, 0.3, 0.0], [0.0, 1.6, 0.0]],
    "combine": "mean",
    "occluder_layers": ["light_occluder"],
}))
.unwrap();
assert_eq!(subject.points.len(), 2);
assert_eq!(subject.facing, Facing::Omni);
assert_eq!(subject.combine, Combine::Mean);
assert_eq!(subject.occluder_layers.unwrap().as_slice(), ["light_occluder"]);
```

